WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.925
Energy sovereignty.

00:00:01.875 --> 00:00:04.113
Landlocked by politics.

00:00:04.113 --> 00:00:10.340
Canada is the world's fourth-largest oil producer and the largest foreign energy supplier to the United States.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:17.943
Yet Canada sells its oil at a discount because it cannot get its energy to tidewater — to global markets where it would command world prices.

00:00:17.943 --> 00:00:28.181
Decades of pipeline politics killed the Northern Gateway pipeline, delayed the Trans Mountain expansion for over a decade, and left Canada dependent on one customer for its largest export.

00:00:28.181 --> 00:00:35.999
The WCS discount — the difference between Western Canadian Select and world prices — has cost Canada billions in foregone revenue.

00:00:35.999 --> 00:00:41.909
The lack of tidewater access means Canada has no alternative when the US changes trade terms.

00:00:41.909 --> 00:00:47.249
The tariff crisis documented on this site is the direct consequence of energy landlocking.

00:00:47.249 --> 00:00:51.397
Three pipeline decisions that determined Canada's energy sovereignty.

00:00:51.397 --> 00:00:54.681
The economic consequence of energy landlocking.

00:00:54.681 --> 00:00:58.909
Energy sovereignty is the economic foundation of national sovereignty.

00:00:58.909 --> 00:01:06.204
A country that produces the world's fourth-largest oil output but can only sell to one customer at a discount is not energy sovereign.

00:01:06.204 --> 00:01:08.272
It is energy captured.

00:01:08.272 --> 00:01:17.147
The pipeline decisions that created this capture were political choices — made through the same captured regulatory and political processes documented across this site.

00:01:17.147 --> 00:01:24.022
The CER is captured by industry but also by environmental and political interests that prevented tidewater access.

00:01:24.022 --> 00:01:34.943
The result serves no one's long-term interest: producers get less revenue, governments get less royalties, workers get fewer jobs, and Canada has no leverage when its only customer changes terms.
